• applies the same principles to pumped storage facilities as to batteries, in accordance with the European Taxonomy recommendations. Accordingly, electricity consumption corresponds to the difference between electricity supplied by the network and that returned to the network. The latter, as a result, is no longer accounted for under the Group’s electricity production;

– for “upstream emissions of purchased fuels and electricity”: the Group uses all volumes of fuels consumed or sold by consolidated entities (Scope 1 combustion and category 3.11 use of sold products); as well as all volumes of energy consumed or lost by consolidated entities (Scope 2),

• applies for Scope 2 the location-based method (quanti ˱cation based on average emission factors of energy production for de˱ned geographical locations) and the market-based method (quanti˱cation based on GHG emissions emitted by producers from which the Group contractually purchases either electricity bundled with instruments or contractual instruments alone);

• discloses biogenic emissions of CO 2 from the combustion or biodegradation of biomass separately from Scope 2 GHG emissions, where applicable;

– for “Generation of purchased electricity that is sold to end users (3.3.D.)”: the Group uses all volumes of energy (electricity, heating, cooling) sold to end-users, distinguishing between the types of commodities (renewable, decarbonized or from the grid). To avoid double counting with GHG emissions related to energy production, country-by-country netting is performed to limit reporting to emissions related to the sale of energy not produced by Group installations,

• does not include any removals, or any purchased, sold or transferred carbon credits or GHG allowances in the calculation of Scope 2 GHG emissions.

Social protection

• retirement: the Group uses a unique digital tool to map, benchmark and evaluate its social protection and pension plans, facilitating the pooling and as such the optimization of these programs. The entities also have access to international insurance networks which provide optimized subscription options, with the potential to share local and global surpluses;

• social protection: if a departing employee is entitled to unemployment insurance bene ˱ts, the cost of the transfer of social security and health guarantees is borne by the active employees’ insurance. The price is calculated accordingly by the insurance company, meaning that the risk is completely under control. Retired employees are no longer eligible for coverage once they retire. While they can request to maintain their health care plan, they are responsible for the full cost. There is therefore no associated ˱nancial risk for ENGIE.

Pay arrangements

• Group employee savings plans: ENGIE mainly uses dedicated funds with strong involvement in the joint governance of the supervisory boards of the various funds;

• ENGIE Care program: in 2020, ENGIE launched its ENGIE Care program, establishing a minimum level of social protection for all employees, regardless of their status, employer or the country to which they belong. It was integrated into the Global Framework Agreement on Fundamental Rights and Social Responsibility in 2022.

Implemented progressively, ENGIE Care makes it possible to raise the level of social protection for its employees and contributes to their retention.

All Group personnel are eligible for:

• savings plans: in France, the Group’s employees have had access to a single Group Savings Plan (Plan d’Épargne Groupe – PEG) since the end of 2009. At the end of 2025, the plan had assets of more than €2.29 billion (compared to €1.77 billion in 2024). Provisions have also been introduced in certain countries outside of France, allowing salaried employees to save under terms adapted to local laws;

• health coverage guaranteeing reimbursement of at least 75% of costs in the event of hospitalization;

• protection for the employee’s family or dependents in the event of death, through the payment of a lump‑sum bene ˱t equivalent to a minimum of 12 months’ salary;

• payment of a capital amount equivalent to at least 12 months’ salary, in the event of total and permanent disability to work;

• retirement savings plans: since 2010, all employees in France have been able to build funds for retirement at their own pace through contributions to the Collective Retirement Savings Plan (Plan d’Épargne pour la Retraite Collectif – PERCOL). Since 2024, it has been possible to invest any value-sharing bonuses in it. Outside France, plans exist in some countries that allow employees to add to their pensions by making voluntary contributions on favorable terms;

• full wage paid for a minimum of 14 weeks in the event of maternity leave and a minimum of four weeks for paternity leave.

A ˱fth pillar, built in 2025, relating to systems promoting well-being, will be deployed progressively from 2026. It aims to:

• promote the physical health of ENGIE employees through a digital questionnaire on health topics, followed by a personalized report to raise participants’ awareness of their health status;

• pro ˱t-sharing and incentive plans: due to the coexistence of separate legal companies, collective variable compensation systems are developed in the subsidiaries. In 2025, the overall volume of employee pro ˱t-sharing, incentive plans and employer contributions (in the event of allocation to both the PEG and the PERCOL) of the different French subsidiaries was more than €180 million (similar to 2024);

• strengthen support for the mental health of employees and their families by offering access to well-being resources and individual psychological support sessions through a dedicated digital platform.

[S1-4 38d] ENGIE has set up monitoring bodies to ensure the effectiveness of these systems and their positive impact on employees:

• the joint committees set up within the Group PERCOL and PERO (Plan d’Épargne Retraite Obligatoire – mandatory company retirement savings plan) ensure that returns are high and sustainable;

• employee share ownership: in 2025, 4% of the Group’s shares were held by employees (as in 2024). Details of the shares they hold, directly or indirectly, are provided in Section 5.4.2.2 Breakdown of shares held directly and indirectly by employees. In 2025, to celebrate the tenth anniversary of the ENGIE brand – 10 years of commitment to energy transition fueled by 150 years of history – the Group implemented an exceptional LINK plan. 42% of eligible employees across the Group subscribed, an increase compared with the 2024 edition (+35%). ENGIE’s contribution under the LINK program was €32.5 million (compared to €13.8 million in 2024);

• monitoring of compliance with the ENGIE Care framework is addressed annually as part of the Global Forum, in order to assess its consistency and the ways in which the program is implemented in practice and at local level for all employees;

• performance shares and long-term incentives: ENGIE grants performance shares, as described in Section 4.2.6 Award of Performance Shares. These shares, which have a vesting period of three years, are subject to internal and external performance conditions. The plan is not limited to senior executives, and nearly 5,200 employees worldwide have bene ˱ted from it (similar to 2024). The 2025 plan includes performance conditions aligned with ENGIE’s strategy and ambitions, especially regarding ESG performance (30% of the total performance conditions);

• for the French entities’ pension and health insurance plans, all insurance contracts are reinsured by the Group’s reinsurer so that the results are pooled at Group level. Together with the Group Bene ˱ts Department, the reinsurance broker ensures that the income statements of the plans are reported in a true and fair manner, and that the annual price reviews are carried out under the best possible conditions, so that employees and companies pay the right price for their cover;

