They also make it possible to construct cross-entity action plans to respond to common challenges, in particular the acquisition of new skills by recruiting new graduates through graduate programs.

Group talent development and retention

[S1-4 38a] In 2024, the Group launched its new employee development strategy with an internally designed and led training day attended by more than 6,000 managers. This training was rolled out to all Group managers in 2025; an e‑learning course was designed for all new managers and will be shared with all employees by the end of 2026. This strategy reinforces managers’ role in supporting Group employees in their personal and career development projects.

[S1-4 38b, c] In 2025, more than 800 positions were identi ˱ed as Global Key Positions, i.e., critical at Group level (compared to 1,100 in 2024). 87% of these positions have a succession plan in place (compared to 81% in 2024). In total, some 2,000 successors (compared to 2,046 in 2024) were identi˱ed among more than 34,000 employees (approximately 22,000 managers and more than 12,000 non-managers) whose potential was assessed by managers during the People Reviews conducted in 2025 (compared to 21,566 managers and 17,989 non-mangers in 2024). Clear action plans have been drawn up to support the development and careers of these successors, as well as other high-potential employees and those planned for mobility next year.

